  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
        "intro": "When brackets around a product or a fraction are raised to a power, that power belongs to every factor inside, the coefficient included. Writing $(5b)^{2}$ as $5b\\times 5b$ shows why: both the $5$ and the $b$ appear twice.",
        "facts": [
          "**Power of a product:** $(ab)^{m}=a^{m}b^{m}$ — the index applies to every factor inside the brackets.",
          "**Power of a quotient:** $\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)^{m}=\\frac{a^{m}}{b^{m}}$ — the index applies to the numerator and the denominator.",
          "A coefficient is a factor, so it must be raised too: $(5b)^{2}=5^{2}b^{2}=25b^{2}$, **not** $5b^{2}$.",
          "Where a factor already carries an index, the two indices **multiply**: $\\left(3x^{2}\\right)^{4}=3^{4}x^{2\\times4}=81x^{8}$.",
          "A negative inside the brackets is raised as well: $(-2x)^{2}=4x^{2}$ but $(-2x)^{3}=-8x^{3}$.",
          "A minus sign **outside** the brackets is not raised, so $-(2x)^{2}=-4x^{2}$.",
          "Anything multiplying the brackets from outside stays as it is: $3\\left(a^{2}b\\right)^{3}=3a^{6}b^{3}$."
        ],
        "steps": [
          "Give the outside index to every factor inside the brackets, keeping any negative sign with its coefficient.",
          "Multiply the indices wherever a factor already has one.",
          "Evaluate the numerical powers and write the simplified expression."
        ]
      },
